            <ArticleTitle>Expression of Interleukin-1 and temporomandibular disorder: Contemporary review of the literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/08869634.2017.1342890</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Temporomandibular disorders (TMD) are a group of conditions affecting the temporomandibular joint (TMJ), leading to jaw dysfunction, joint and muscle pain, and a decrease in quality of life. A communication network of pro- and anti-inflammatory mediators called cytokines maintains the homeostasis of the TMJ. This review will focus on the Interleukin (IL) family of cytokines, which have been quantified in TMJ synovial fluids in a variety of studies. IL-1α and IL-1β have pro-inflammatory effects, while the endogenous receptor antagonist (IL-1RA) inhibits the pro-inflammatory effects of IL-1.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A literature search (2006-2016) to identify eligible studies was completed using the PubMed database. Studies identified used saline irrigation to quantify cytokine profiles in synovial fluid of healthy and/or dysfunctional joints.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The initial search yielded 111 articles, 5 of which met the inclusion criteria after inter-reviewer discussion.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Articles that compared IL-1 concentrations in TMD vs. control groups found significant differences.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
